\[
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos 2\theta & \sin 2\theta \\
\sin 2\theta & -\cos 2\theta
\end{pmatrix}
\]

Comparing this with the given matrix, we find that \(\cos 2\theta = \frac{7}{25}\) and \(\sin 2\theta = -\frac{24}{25}\). This implies that \(2\theta\) is in the fourth quadrant.

